raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:482:<li class="level-1">Learn about the developmental milestones that most babies do by two months of age.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:483:<li class="level-1">See important information to share with your doctor.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:484:<li class="level-1">Find tips and activities you can do for your two-month-old.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:501:	<a class="onThisPageAnchor" id="cdc_generic_section_2-ways-to-monitor-milestones" name="Ways%20to%20monitor%20milestones" title="Ways to monitor milestones"><!-- --></a><h2 class="editor-block--title" id="heading-wzvbrfydix">Ways to monitor milestones</h2>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:502:<p>How your child plays, learns, speaks, acts, and moves offers important clues about your child's development. Developmental milestones are things most children (75% or more) can do by a certain age.</p>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:504:<p>Check the milestones your baby has reached by 2 months by completing a checklist, available below and in three additional formats:</p>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:505:<ul class="nested-list">
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:506:<li class="level-1">CDC's free <a href="/act-early/milestones-app/index.html"><em>Milestone Tracker</em> mobile app</a>, for iOS and Android devices</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:507:<li class="level-1"><a href="/act-early/digital-online-checklist/2-months.html" class="">Web-based Digital Online Checklist</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:508:<li class="level-1"><a href="/act-early/media/pdfs/2025/05/Eng.-2-months-Milestone-Moments-Checklist-2021-P.pdf" class="ce-selection" target="_blank">Printable checklist<span aria-label=" pdf icon" role="img" class="sr-only"></span><span class="cdc-link-icon cdc-link-icon--pdf" aria-hidden="true"></span></a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:518:<p><strong>Click the links below to view a photo or video of each milestone.</strong></p>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:519:<div class="dfe-block dfe-block--cdcmodule cdc_raw_html dfe-block--width-standard"><div class="dfe-block__inner"><div class="dfe-block dfe-block--cdcmodule cdc_raw_html"><h3 class="mt-0">Social/Emotional Milestones</h3>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:520:<ul>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:521: 	<li><a href="#calmsDown" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#calmsDown">Calms down when spoken to or picked up</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:522: 	<li><a href="#looksFace" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#looksFace">Looks at your face </a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:523: 	<li><a href="#seemsHappy" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#seemsHappy">Seems happy to see you when you walk up to her</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:524:	<li><a href="#smilesTalk2" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#smilesTalk2">Smiles when you talk to or smile at her</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:527:<ul>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:528: 	<li> <a href="#Makessoundsotherthancrying" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#Makessoundsotherthancrying">Makes sounds other than crying</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:529: 	<li><a href="#Reactstoloudsounds" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#Reactstoloudsounds">Reacts to loud sounds</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:532:<h3>Cognitive Milestones (learning, thinking, problem-solving)</h3>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:533:<ul>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:534: 	<li><a href="#watchesYouMove" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#watchesYouMove">Watches you as you move</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:535: 	<li><a href="#looksToy" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#looksToy">Looks at a toy for several seconds</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:537:<h3>Movement/Physical Development Milestones</h3>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:538:<ul>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:539: 	<li><a href="#holdsHeadUp" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#holdsHeadUp">Holds head up when on tummy</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:540: 	<li><a href="#Movesbotharms" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#Movesbotharms">Moves both arms and both legs</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:541: 	<li><a href="#openshandsbriefly" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-bs-toggle="modal" data-bs-target="#openshandsbriefly">Opens hands briefly</a></li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:548:<ul class="nested-list">
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:549:<li class="level-1">What are some things you and your baby do together?</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:550:<li class="level-1">What are some things your baby likes to do?</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:551:<li class="level-1">Is there anything your baby does or does not do that concerns you?</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:552:<li class="level-1">Has your baby lost any skills he/she once had?</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:553:<li class="level-1">Does your baby have any special healthcare needs or was he/she born prematurely?</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:555:<p><strong>You know your child best. </strong>Don't wait. If your child is not meeting one or more milestones, has lost skills he or she once had, or you have other concerns, act early. Talk with your child's doctor, share your concerns, and ask about developmental screening.</p>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:558:<div class="cdc-callout__body"><div class="cdc-callout__content"><ul><li>Ask for a referral to a specialist who can evaluate your child more; and</li><li>Call your state or territory's early intervention program to find out if your child can get services to help. Learn more and find the number at <a href="/act-early/early-intervention/contact-information-by-state.html" class="">cdc.gov/FindEI</a>.</li></ul></div>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:569:<ul class="nested-list">
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:570:<li class="level-1">Respond positively to your baby. Act excited, smile, and talk to him when he makes sounds. This teaches him to take turns "talking" back and forth in conversation.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:571:<li class="level-1">Talk, read, and sing to your baby to help her develop and understand language.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:572:<li class="level-1">Spend time cuddling and holding your baby. This will help him feel safe and cared for. You will not spoil your baby by holding or responding to him.</li>
raw/cdc/legacy-2mo.html:576:<div class="cdc-callout__body"><div class="cdc-callout__content"><ul><li>Being responsive to your baby helps him learn and grow. Limiting your screen time when you are with your baby helps you be responsive.</li><li>Take care of yourself. Parenting can be hard work! It's easier to enjoy your new baby when you feel good yourself.</li><li>Learn to notice and respond to your baby's signals to know what she's feeling and needs. You will feel good and your baby will feel safe and loved. For example, is she trying to "play" with you by making sounds and looking at you, or is she turning her head away, yawning, or becoming fussy because she needs a break?</li><li>Lay your baby on his tummy when he is awake and put toys at eye level in front of him. This will help him practice lifting his head up. Do not leave your baby alone. If he seems sleepy, place him on his back in a safe sleep area (firm mattress with no blankets, pillows, bumper pads, or toys).</li><li>Feed only breast milk or formula to your baby. Babies are not ready for other foods, water or other drinks for about the first 6 months of life.</li><li>Learn when your baby is hungry by looking for signs. Watch for signs of hunger, such as putting hands to mouth, turning head toward breast/bottle, or smacking/licking lips.</li><li>Look for signs your baby is full, such as closing her mouth or turning her head away from the breast/bottle. If your baby is not hungry, it's ok to stop feeding.</li><li>Do not shake your baby or allow anyone else to&mdash;ever! You can damage his brain or even cause his death. Put your baby in a safe place and walk away if you're getting upset when he is crying. Check on him every 5&ndash;10 minutes. Infant crying is often worse in the first few months of life, but it gets better!</li><li>Have routines for sleeping and feeding. This will help your baby begin to learn what to expect.</li><li>Hold a rattle off to one side of your baby's head, shake it, and see if your baby looks for the noise.</li><li>Practice staying calm when your baby is upset by talking softly, holding, rocking, or singing to her. This will help her calm down and lessen her stress and fears.</li><li>Limit screen time (TV, tablets, phones, etc.) to video calling with loved ones. Screen time is not recommended for children younger than 2 years of age. Babies learn by talking, playing, and interacting with others.</li><li>Talk and play with your baby while feeding, dressing, and bathing.</li><li>Help your baby learn some ways to self-soothe by letting her suck on her fingers or a pacifier.</li><li>Copy your baby's sounds and see how long your baby "talks" by making sounds back and forth with you.</li><li>Place a baby-safe mirror near your baby so she can look at herself. She will begin to develop a sense of who she is.</li><li>Look at pictures with bright colors or faces with your baby and talk about them.</li></ul></div>
